---
// Data Size & Storage Unit Converter — convert a value between bit, byte,
// the decimal (SI) size prefixes (kB-PB, ×1000 per step), and the binary
// (IEC) size prefixes (KiB-PiB, ×1024 per step) live as you type. Pure
// floating-point arithmetic through a fixed, NIST-verified multiplier table
// (src/utils/dataSize.ts) — nothing here needs runtime lookup, it's unit
// conversion. Same page shape as the other interactive tools (intro aside ->
// tool -> reference notes). IT-fundamentals content, not DFIR-specific — see
// consts.ts's Domain notes for
// the framing rule this page follows. Static page — prerender to HTML
// served from the CDN.
export const prerender = true;
import { css } from 'styled-system/css';
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import PageHeader from '../../components/PageHeader.astro';
import Icon from '../../components/Icon.astro';
import DataSizeConverter from '../../components/DataSizeConverter.astro';
import { toolChip } from '../../components/RelatedTools.astro';
import ExternalResources from '../../components/ExternalResources.astro';
import { EXTERNAL_RESOURCES } from '../../data/externalResources';
import { DATA_UNITS, convertDataSize, formatDataValue } from '../../utils/dataSize';

const sectionH2 = css({
  display: 'flex',
  alignItems: 'center',
  gap: '0.6rem',
  fontSize: '1.3rem',
  fontWeight: 700,
  mt: '2.5rem',
  mb: '1rem',
  pt: '1.5rem',
  borderTop: '1px solid token(colors.border)',
  scrollMarginTop: '90px',
});
const refTable = css({ width: '100%', borderCollapse: 'collapse', fontSize: '0.85rem' });
const refTh = css({
  textAlign: 'left',
  color: 'textMuted',
  fontFamily: 'sans',
  fontWeight: 600,
  fontSize: '0.72rem',
  textTransform: 'uppercase',
  letterSpacing: '0.06em',
  p: '0.5rem 0.7rem',
  borderBottom: '1px solid token(colors.border)',
});
const refTd = css({ p: '0.5rem 0.7rem', borderBottom: '1px solid token(colors.border)', color: 'text', verticalAlign: 'top' });
const refTdMuted = css({
  p: '0.5rem 0.7rem',
  borderBottom: '1px solid token(colors.border)',
  color: 'textMuted',
  fontSize: '0.8rem',
  verticalAlign: 'top',
});
const relatedList = css({ display: 'flex', flexDirection: 'column', gap: '0.85rem' });
const relatedReason = css({ color: 'textMuted', fontSize: '0.85rem', mt: '0.35rem', lineHeight: 1.6 });
const inlineCode = css({ fontFamily: 'mono', fontSize: '0.92em', color: 'primary' });
const calloutBox = css({
  bg: 'bgSubtle',
  border: '1px solid token(colors.border)',
  borderLeftWidth: '3px',
  borderLeftColor: 'accent',
  borderRadius: 'md',
  p: '1rem 1.25rem',
  mt: '1.5rem',
});

// The worked example the page's copy walks through and the converter loads
// with by default — computed here (not hand-typed in markup) so the prose
// below is guaranteed to match what src/utils/dataSize.ts actually returns.
// See test/dataSize.test.ts's own "worked example" tests for the same figures.
const example500GB = convertDataSize(500, 'GB');
const example5TB = convertDataSize(5, 'TB');
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Data Size & Storage Unit Converter"
  description="Convert a value between bit, byte, decimal (kB-PB) and binary (KiB-PiB) size units live as you type — and see exactly why a '500 GB' drive shows less in Windows."
>
  <PageHeader
    breadcrumbs={[{ label: 'Home', href: '/' }, { label: 'Tools', href: '/tools/' }, { label: 'Data Size & Storage Unit Converter' }]}
    eyebrow="Tools"
    title="Data Size & Storage Unit Converter"
    description="Type a value into any unit — bit, byte, decimal kB-PB, or binary KiB-PiB — and every other field updates live, so you can see exactly how the two prefix systems diverge."
  />

  <aside
    class={css({
      bg: 'bgSubtle',
      border: '1px solid token(colors.border)',
      borderLeftWidth: '3px',
      borderLeftColor: 'primary',
      borderRadius: 'md',
      p: '1rem 1.25rem',
      mb: '1.5rem',
    })}
  >
    <p class={css({ color: 'text', lineHeight: 1.7, fontSize: '0.95rem', m: 0 })}>
      This is plain unit conversion through a fixed, published multiplier table — nothing here is looked up or
      estimated, and it all happens locally, so nothing you type ever leaves your machine.
    </p>
  </aside>

  <p class={css({ color: 'text', lineHeight: 1.8, fontSize: '1rem', mb: '1rem' })}>
    There are two entirely different, both-legitimate ways to define "kilobyte," and computing has never fully
    settled on one. Storage manufacturers — hard drive, SSD, USB drive, and memory-card makers — label capacity
    in the plain decimal sense: a kilobyte is 1,000 bytes, a megabyte is 1,000,000, and so on, the same way "kilo"
    means 1,000 of anything else in the metric system. Operating systems and most software, on the other hand,
    have traditionally reported storage in binary: because memory addressing is inherently a power of two, a
    "kilobyte" there has usually meant 1,024 bytes, a "megabyte" 1,048,576, and so on — close to the decimal
    values, but not the same number.
  </p>
  <p class={css({ color: 'text', lineHeight: 1.8, fontSize: '1rem', mb: '1.5rem' })}>
    That mismatch is exactly why a drive labeled "500 GB" shows up as roughly{' '}
    <strong class={css({ color: 'text' })}>{formatDataValue(example500GB.GiB, 5)} GB</strong> in Windows' own
    binary-based reporting — the drive has the full, honestly-labeled 500,000,000,000 bytes, it's just that
    Windows is dividing that same byte count by 1,073,741,824 (2<sup>30</sup>) instead of 1,000,000,000. To remove
    the ambiguity, the IEC standardized a separate set of prefixes for the binary case in 1998 —{' '}
    <strong class={css({ color: 'text' })}>kibi, mebi, gibi, tebi, pebi</strong> (symbols Ki, Mi, Gi, Ti, Pi) — so
    "KiB" unambiguously means 1,024 bytes and "kB" unambiguously means 1,000, even though most consumer software
    still labels binary sizes "KB"/"MB"/"GB" out of habit. This tool keeps both families — and the base bit/byte
    units — in sync as you type into any one of them, so you can see the real numbers side by side instead of
    doing the arithmetic by hand.
  </p>
  <p class={css({ color: 'text', lineHeight: 1.8, fontSize: '1rem', mb: '1.5rem' })}>
    One more mismatch worth knowing: network speeds are conventionally measured in <em>bits</em> per second, not
    bytes — a "100 Mbps" internet connection is 100 megabits per second, which is only 12.5 megabytes per second
    (divide by 8, since 1 byte = 8 bit), not 100. A file-transfer or download-speed reading in MB/s versus a
    plan's advertised Mbps looking "8x too slow" is almost always just this same bit-vs-byte distinction, not a
    problem with the connection.
  </p>

  <h2 id="calculator" class={css({ display: 'flex', alignItems: 'center', gap: '0.6rem', fontSize: '1.3rem', fontWeight: 700, mb: '1rem', scrollMarginTop: '90px' })}>
    <Icon name="hard-drive" size={20} class={css({ color: 'primary' })} /> Calculator
  </h2>
  <DataSizeConverter />

  <h2 id="reference" class={sectionH2}>
    <Icon name="list" size={20} class={css({ color: 'primary' })} /> Unit reference
  </h2>
  <div class={css({ overflowX: 'auto' })} tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Unit reference table">
    <table class={refTable}>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th class={refTh}>Unit</th>
          <th class={refTh}>System</th>
          <th class={refTh}>Multiplier</th>
          <th class={refTh}>Bytes</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {
          DATA_UNITS.map((u) => (
            <tr>
              <td class={refTd}>
                <strong class={css({ color: 'text' })}>
                  {u.label} ({u.symbol})
                </strong>
              </td>
              <td class={refTdMuted}>
                {u.system === 'base' && 'Base unit'}
                {u.system === 'decimal' && 'Decimal (SI)'}
                {u.system === 'binary' && 'Binary (IEC)'}
              </td>
              <td class={refTdMuted}>
                {u.id === 'bit' && '1/8'}
                {u.id === 'byte' && '1'}
                {u.system === 'decimal' && `1000^${Math.round(Math.log10(u.bytesPerUnit) / 3)}`}
                {u.system === 'binary' && `1024^${Math.round(Math.log2(u.bytesPerUnit) / 10)}`}
              </td>
              <td class={refTdMuted}>{formatDataValue(u.bytesPerUnit, 16)}</td>
            </tr>
          ))
        }
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <div class={calloutBox}>
    <p class={css({ color: 'text', lineHeight: 1.7, fontSize: '0.92rem', m: 0, mb: '0.6rem' })}>
      <strong>Worked example.</strong> A "500 GB" hard drive, labeled the way every manufacturer labels drives:
    </p>
    <p class={css({ color: 'textMuted', fontSize: '0.88rem', lineHeight: 1.8, m: 0 })}>
      500 GB (decimal) = 500 × 1,000,000,000 = <code class={inlineCode}>{Math.round(example500GB.byte).toLocaleString('en-US')}</code>{' '}
      bytes exactly.
      <br />
      That same byte count, read back in binary units: {formatDataValue(example500GB.GiB, 6)} GiB — which is why file
      managers and Windows' own "Properties" dialog show a "500 GB" drive as roughly{' '}
      {Math.trunc(example500GB.GiB)} GB, not 500. Nothing is missing; it's the same bytes, reported in a different
      base.
      <br />
      The same math applies at every size — a "5 TB" drive is {formatDataValue(example5TB.TiB, 6)} TiB in binary
      terms.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="related" class={sectionH2}>
    <Icon name="wrench" size={20} class={css({ color: 'primary' })} /> Related tools
  </h2>
  <div class={relatedList}>
    <div>
      <a href="/tools/base-converter/" class={toolChip}>
        <Icon name="hash" size={13} /> Number Base Converter <Icon name="arrow-right" size={13} />
      </a>
      <p class={relatedReason}>
        A byte is exactly two hex digits and eight binary digits — this converter handles the size-prefix math,
        that one handles reading the raw binary/hex values a byte count is ultimately made of.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div>
      <a href="/reference/osi-model/" class={toolChip}>
        <Icon name="layers" size={13} /> OSI &amp; TCP/IP Model Reference <Icon name="arrow-right" size={13} />
      </a>
      <p class={relatedReason}>
        Covers where the bit-vs-byte distinction shows up in networking (link speeds in bits per second) alongside
        the rest of the model those speeds are measured across.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <ExternalResources resources={EXTERNAL_RESOURCES['data-size-converter']} />
</BaseLayout>
